-- Rules that call YMD, from the daydate library. `due` answers the date YMD
-- gives: a date for a month and a day that exist in that year, and YMD's
-- refusal, with its reason, for a month or a day that does not. `overdue`
-- calls YMD on a date that exists, so it answers on every input, and core,
-- the service, the REPL and the language server decline to plan it even so,
-- with both of YMD's reasons. See boolean-rules.l4 for what an "agreement:"
-- line says, and for `pad`.
--
-- agreement: due may refuse with ["YMD: that month has no such day in that year", "YMD: the month is not a whole number from 1 to 12"]
-- agreement: due {"year": 2024, "month": 2, "day": 29} => answers "2024-02-29"
-- agreement: due {"year": 2026, "month": 13, "day": 1} => refuses "YMD: the month is not a whole number from 1 to 12"
-- agreement: due {"year": 2023, "month": 2, "day": 29} => refuses "YMD: that month has no such day in that year"
--
-- agreement: overdue may refuse with ["YMD: that month has no such day in that year", "YMD: the month is not a whole number from 1 to 12"]
-- agreement: overdue {"late": true} => answers true
-- agreement: overdue {"late": false} => answers false

IMPORT daydate

@export the date a payment is due
GIVEN year IS A NUMBER
      month IS A NUMBER
      day IS A NUMBER
      pad IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
due year month day pad MEANS YMD year month day

@export whether a payment due on 28 July 2026 is overdue
GIVEN late IS A BOOLEAN
      pad IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE overdue IF late AND DATE_DAY (YMD 2026 7 28) EQUALS 28
